\subsection{Key Components}

\begin{description}
  \item[LLVM IR] A typed, SSA-based intermediate representation. Supports three equivalent forms: in-memory (C++ API), human-readable (.ll files), and compact (.bc bitcode files).
  \item[Pass Manager] Orchestrates the execution of optimization passes. Supports function passes, module passes, and loop passes.
  \item[Target Framework] Describes target architectures (registers, instructions, calling conventions) using TableGen descriptions.
  \item[Analysis Framework] Provides standard analyses (dominance, loop info, alias analysis) that passes can use.
\end{description}

\subsection{Writing LLVM Passes}

LLVM passes are the primary extension mechanism. A pass transforms or analyzes LLVM IR:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Example: a simple LLVM pass that counts instructions
  struct CountInsts : public PassInfoMixin<CountInsts> {
    PreservedAnalyses run(Function &F, FunctionAnalysisManager &AM) {
      unsigned count = 0;
      for (auto &BB : F)
        for (auto &I : BB)
          count++;
      errs() << F.getName() << ": " << count << " instructions\n";
      return PreservedAnalyses::all();
    }
  };
\end{verbatim}

\section{GCC Internals}\label{sec:gcc}

\subsection{Overview}

The \emph{GNU Compiler Collection} (GCC) is the oldest widely-used retargetable compiler. Originally written for C, it now supports C, C++, Fortran, Ada, Go, D, and other languages.

\subsection{Architecture}

GCC's architecture has evolved over decades. Modern GCC (version 10+) uses:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{GENERIC}: A language-independent IR shared by all front ends.
  \item \textbf{GIMPLE}: A three-address, SSA-based IR derived from GENERIC.
  \item \textbf{RTL} (Register Transfer Language): A low-level IR close to machine instructions.
  \item \textbf{Machine descriptions}: Table-driven descriptions of target architectures.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key Differences from LLVM}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{GCC vs.\ LLVM}
\label{tab:gcc-vs-llvm}
\begin{tabular}{lp{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{GCC} & \textbf{LLVM} \\
\hline
IR & GIMPLE (3-address) + RTL & LLVM IR (SSA, typed) \\
License & GPL (copyleft) & Apache 2.0 with LLVM Exceptions \\
Link-time opt & LTO (since GCC 4.5) & LTO (since early versions) \\
Extensibility & Plugins, passes & Passes, C++ API \\
Compilation speed & Slower & Faster \\
Code quality & Competitive & Competitive \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{MLIR Ecosystem}\label{sec:mlir}

\subsection{Overview}

\emph{MLIR} (Multi-Level Intermediate Representation) is a framework for building reusable, extensible compiler infrastructure. It was developed at Google and open-sourced in 2019.

\subsection{Key Concepts}

\begin{description}
  \item[Dialects] Self-contained sets of operations, types, and attributes. Dialects form a hierarchy from high-level (close to source) to low-level (close to hardware).
  \item[Operations] The fundamental unit of computation. Each operation has a name, operands, results, attributes, and regions.
  \item[Regions and Blocks] Operations can contain regions, which are sequences of basic blocks. This enables structured representation of control flow.
  \item[Rewrite Patterns] Transformations are expressed as pattern-matching rewrite rules.
\end{description}

\subsection{Key Dialects}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Affine}: Loop nests with affine constraints. Enables polyhedral optimizations.
  \item \textbf{Linalg}: Linear algebra operations. Bridge between high-level ML and low-level computation.
  \item \textbf{SCF} (Structured Control Flow): \texttt{for}, \texttt{if}, \texttt{while} constructs.
  \item \textbf{Vector}: Vector operations for SIMD code generation.
  \item \textbf{GPU}: GPU-specific operations for CUDA, OpenCL, Vulkan.
  \item \textbf{LLVM}: Low-level operations that map directly to LLVM IR.
\end{itemize}

\section{OCaml Compiler Internals}\label{sec:ocaml}

\subsection{Overview}

The \emph{OCaml} compiler is a self-hosting compiler for the OCaml language. It produces native code for multiple architectures and bytecode for the OCaml virtual machine.

\subsection{Compilation Pipeline}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Parsing}: Lexer (ocamllex) and parser (menhir/ocamlyacc) produce an AST.
  \item \textbf{Typing}: The type checker performs Hindley--Milner type inference with extensions (GADTs, first-class modules, etc.).
  \item \textbf{Lambda}: The typed AST is translated to a lambda calculus IR.
  \item \textbf{Closure conversion}: Higher-order functions are converted to closures.
  \item \textbf{Cmm}: A low-level IR (C--like) with explicit memory allocation.
  \item \textbf{Selection}: Instructions are selected for the target architecture.
  \item \textbf{Scheduling}: Instructions are scheduled for the target pipeline.
  \item \textbf{Register allocation}: Graph-coloring register allocation.
  \item \textbf{Emission}: Assembly code is emitted.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key Features for Compiler Writers}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Pattern matching}: OCaml's pattern matching is ideal for implementing compilers.
  \item \textbf{Algebraic data types}: ASTs and IRs are naturally expressed as algebraic data types.
  \item \textbf{Module system}: Large compiler implementations benefit from OCaml's module system.
  \item \textbf{Performance}: The native-code compiler produces efficient code, making OCaml suitable for self-hosting.
\end{itemize}

\section{GHC Implementation}\label{sec:ghc}

\subsection{Overview}

The \emph{Glasgow Haskell Compiler} (GHC) is the standard Haskell compiler. It is a sophisticated, self-hosting compiler that implements many advanced Haskell features.

\subsection{Compilation Pipeline}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Parsing}: The parser produces an AST.
  \item \textbf{Renaming}: Names are resolved and disambiguated.
  \item \textbf{Type checking}: The type checker performs type inference with type classes, GADTs, type families, and more.
  \item \textbf{Desugaring}: Syntactic sugar is eliminated (pattern matching, do-notation, etc.).
  \item \textbf{Core}: The program is translated to \emph{Core}, a small, typed intermediate language (System FC).
  \item \textbf{Optimization}: Core-to-Core transformations (simplification, specialization, float-in/out, etc.).
  \item \textbf{STG}: Core is translated to the \emph{Spineless Tagless G-machine} (STG) IR.
  \item \textbf{Cmm}: STG is translated to C-- (a low-level IR).
  \item \textbf{Code generation}: Cmm is translated to assembly or LLVM IR.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Core: The Heart of GHC}

\emph{Core} is GHC's central intermediate representation. It is a small, typed, functional language based on System FC (a variant of System $F_\omega$ with coercions).

\begin{grammar}{GHC Core}
  \production{e}{x \mid c \mid \lambda x.\,e \mid e_1\;e_2 \mid \mathbf{let}\;x = e_1\;\mathbf{in}\;e_2 \mid \mathbf{case}\;e\;\mathbf{of}\;\{\ldots\} \mid \Lambda \alpha.\,e \mid e\;[\tau]}
\end{grammar}

All of GHC's optimization passes operate on Core. This makes Core the most important IR to understand for GHC developers.

\section{V8 and SpiderMonkey}\label{sec:js-engines}

\subsection{V8 (Google Chrome / Node.js)}

\emph{V8} is Google's JavaScript engine, used in Chrome and Node.js. Its architecture:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Parser}: Produces an AST from JavaScript source.
  \item \textbf{Ignition}: A register-based bytecode interpreter. Fast startup, collects profiling data.
  \item \textbf{Maglev}: A mid-tier optimizing compiler. Uses the profiling data from Ignition.
  \item \textbf{TurboFan}: A graph-based optimizing compiler. Produces highly optimized machine code.
  \item \textbf{Maglev/TurboFan deoptimization}: If assumptions are violated, optimized code is replaced with interpreter code.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{SpiderMonkey (Mozilla Firefox)}

\emph{SpiderMonkey} is Mozilla's JavaScript engine. Its architecture:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Parser}: Produces an AST (Bytecode).
  \item \textbf{Baseline}: A baseline JIT compiler. Fast compilation, moderate performance.
  \item \textbf{IonMonkey}: An optimizing JIT compiler. Performs SSA-based optimization.
  \item \textbf{Warp}: A newer approach that uses CacheIR for inline caching.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Truffle/GraalVM}\label{sec:truffle-graalvm}

\subsection{Overview}

\emph{Truffle} is a language implementation framework built on top of the \emph{GraalVM}. It allows language implementers to write interpreters in Java, and the GraalVM JIT compiler automatically generates efficient machine code.

\subsection{How It Works}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The language implementer writes an AST interpreter in Java, using Truffle's node framework.
  \item Each AST node is annotated with profiling information (type feedback, execution counts).
  \item The GraalVM JIT compiler (Graal) uses partial evaluation to compile the interpreter into machine code.
  \item The result is performance comparable to a hand-written compiler.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{insight}
Truffle/GraalVM realizes the first Futamura projection (Chapter~\ref{ch:futamura}) in practice: partial evaluation of the interpreter yields compiled code. The key innovation is that the partial evaluation is performed by the JIT compiler, using profiling data gathered during interpretation.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Languages Implemented with Truffle}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{GraalPy}: A Python implementation.
  \item \textbf{TruffleRuby}: A Ruby implementation.
  \item \textbf{Graal.js}: A JavaScript implementation (alternative to V8).
  \item \textbf{Sulong}: A C implementation.
  \item \textbf{SimpleLanguage}: A tutorial language for learning Truffle.
\end{itemize}

\section{Proof Assistants: Coq, Agda, Lean}\label{sec:proof-assistants}

\subsection{Coq}

\emph{Coq} is a proof assistant based on the \emph{Calculus of Inductive Constructions} (CIC), a dependently typed lambda calculus with inductive types and a universal hierarchy.

\begin{description}
  \item[Type theory] Calculus of Inductive Constructions (CIC).
  \item[Key features] Inductive types, pattern matching, dependent types, tactics, extraction to OCaml/Haskell/C.
  \item[Applications] Verified compilers (CompCert), verified operating systems (CertiKOS), mathematical formalization (Four Color Theorem).
\end{description}

\subsection{Agda}

\emph{Agda} is a dependently typed programming language and proof assistant based on \emph{Martin-L\"of type theory}.

\begin{description}
  \item[Type theory] Intensional Martin-L\"of type theory with inductive families.
  \item[Key features] Dependent types, pattern matching, universe polymorphism, cubical type theory (Agda 2.6.2+).
  \item[Applications] Programming language metatheory, formalized mathematics, verified software.
\end{description}

\subsection{Lean}

\emph{Lean} is a theorem prover and functional programming language developed at Microsoft Research.

\begin{description}
  \item[Type theory] The Calculus of Inductive Constructions (Lean 3) / a variant with quotient types and classical axioms (Lean 4).
  \item[Key features] Tactics, metaprogramming (Lean 4 is self-hosted), mathlib (a large mathematical library).
  \item[Applications] Mathematical formalization (the primary use case), verified software.
\end{description}

