\section{Summary}\label{sec:tools-summary}

This chapter has surveyed the major tools and implementations discussed throughout the book. The key takeaways are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{LLVM} is the dominant compiler infrastructure for systems languages.
  \item \textbf{MLIR} is the next frontier, enabling multi-level, cross-domain compilation.
  \item \textbf{OCaml and GHC} are the reference implementations for functional language research.
  \item \textbf{V8 and SpiderMonkey} demonstrate the state of the art in JIT compilation.
  \item \textbf{Truffle/GraalVM} realizes the Futamura projections in practice.
  \item \textbf{Coq, Agda, and Lean} are the tools of choice for verified compilation and formal methods.
\end{enumerate}

The reader is encouraged to explore these tools hands-on. Many of them have extensive documentation, tutorials, and active communities. The best way to understand a compiler architecture is to read its source code, modify it, and observe the results.

\section{Extended Tool Descriptions}\label{sec:tools-extended}

\subsection{LLVM Extended: The New Pass Manager}

LLVM's new pass manager (NPM), introduced in LLVM 13, replaces the legacy pass manager with a more efficient and composable design:

\begin{description}
  \item[Pass Instrumentation] The NPM provides hooks for pass instrumentation: before/after callbacks, timing, and analysis invalidation tracking. This enables profiling of pass execution and identification of optimization bottlenecks.
  \item[Analysis Caching] Analyses (e.g., dominator trees, loop info) are cached and invalidated lazily. When a transformation modifies the IR, only the affected analyses are recomputed.
  \item[Pass Pipeline] The pipeline is described declaratively using a text-based pipeline description language:
\end{description}

\begin{verbatim}
  pipeline = "function(coro-cond,coro-split),\
              function(cgscc(devirt,inline)),\
              globalopt,function(GVN),instcombine"
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{MLIR Extended: Dialect Design}

MLIR's dialect system enables domain-specific intermediate representations. A dialect defines:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Operations}: Custom operations with typed operands and results.
  \item \textbf{Types}: Domain-specific types (e.g., tensor types, quantized types).
  \item \textbf{Attributes}: Compile-time constant data attached to operations.
  \item \textbf{Interfaces}: Polymorphic behavior that operations can implement.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}[Defining a Custom Dialect]
A typical MLIR dialect for linear algebra might define:
\begin{verbatim}
  def MatMulOp : Op<linalg, "matmul"> {
    let arguments = (ins
      AnyRankedOrUnrankedTensor:$A,
      AnyRankedOrUnrankedTensor:$B,
      AnyRankedOrUnrankedTensor:$C
    );
    let results = (outs AnyRankedOrUnrankedTensor:$result);
    let description = [{
      Computes C = A * B + C (matrix multiply-accumulate).
    }];
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{GHC Extended: The STG Machine}

The Glasgow Haskell Compiler (GHC) uses a multi-stage compilation pipeline:

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  >=stealth,
  node distance=1.2cm,
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners, minimum width=2.2cm, minimum height=0.6cm, fill=TomeBg, align=center, font=\small},
]
  \node[box] (hs) {Haskell \\ Source};
  \node[box, right=1.5cm of hs] (core) {Core \\ (typed)};
  \node[box, right=1.5cm of core] (stg) {STG \\ (graph)};
  \node[box, right=1.5cm of stg] (cmm) {Cmm \\ (C--)};
  \node[box, right=1.5cm of cmm] (llvm) {LLVM \\ IR};
  \node[box, right=1.5cm of llvm] (asm) {Assembly};

  \draw[->] (hs) -- node[above, font=\tiny] {desugar} (core);
  \draw[->] (core) -- node[above, font=\tiny] {simplify} (stg);
  \draw[->] (stg) -- node[above, font=\tiny] {code gen} (cmm);
  \draw[->] (cmm) -- node[above, font=\tiny] {lower} (llvm);
  \draw[->] (llvm) -- node[above, font=\tiny] {backend} (asm);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{GHC compilation pipeline from Haskell source to assembly}
\label{fig:ghc-pipeline}
\end{figure}

\begin{description}
  \item[Core] A typed, explicitly-typed lambda calculus (System FC). All Haskell features are desugared into Core: type classes become dictionary passing, GADTs become existential types, and pattern matching becomes case expressions.
  \item[STG (Spineless Tagless G-machine)] A graph-reduction intermediate representation. Lazy evaluation is implemented by representing thunks as closures that are updated in place when evaluated.
  \item[Cmm (C--)] A low-level, portable intermediate language. Supports garbage collection, stack management, and foreign function calls.
  \item[Worker/Wrapper Transformation] GHC's key optimization for strictness analysis: a polymorphic function is split into a \emph{worker} (specialized to unboxed types) and a \emph{wrapper} (the original interface).
\end{description}

\subsection{V8 Extended: TurboFan and Maglev}

The V8 JavaScript engine uses a tiered compilation strategy:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{V8 compilation tiers}
\label{tab:v8-tiers}
\begin{tabular}{llp{6cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Tier} & \textbf{Compiler} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
1 & Ignition & Bytecode interpreter. Fast startup, low memory. Generates feedback for profiling. \\
2 & Maglev & Mid-tier optimizing compiler. Uses Maglev IR (sea-of-nodes). Balances compile time and optimization quality. \\
3 & TurboFan & Full optimizing compiler. Uses TurboFan graph IR (sea-of-nodes with typed nodes). Performs aggressive optimizations: inlining, escape analysis, speculative optimization. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{description}
  \item[Speculative Optimization] TurboFan speculates on types observed at runtime. For example, if a variable has always been a small integer, TurboFan generates code that assumes it will continue to be a small integer, with a deoptimization bailout if the assumption is violated.
  \item[Hidden Classes] V8 uses hidden classes (internally called ``maps'') to optimize property access. Objects with the same allocation pattern share a hidden class, enabling inline caching for property lookups.
  \item[Maglev] Introduced in V8 10.1, Maglev fills the gap between Ignition and TurboFan. It compiles faster than TurboFan but produces better code than Ignition, improving the time-to-optimized-code tradeoff.
\end{description}

\subsection{Coq Extended: The Calculus of Inductive Constructions}

Coq is based on the \emph{Calculus of Inductive Constructions} (CIC), which combines:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The \emph{Calculus of Constructions} (Coquand, 1986): a type theory with dependent types, polymorphism, and a type hierarchy.
  \item \emph{Inductive definitions} (Paulin-Mohring, 1993): a mechanism for defining inductive types (natural numbers, lists, trees) with automatically generated induction principles.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Inductive Definition in Coq]
\begin{verbatim}
  Inductive list (A : Type) : Type :=
    | nil : list A
    | cons : A -> list A -> list A.

  Fixpoint length (A : Type) (l : list A) : nat :=
    match l with
    | nil => 0
    | cons _ t => S (length A t)
    end.

  Theorem length_app : forall (A : Type) (l1 l2 : list A),
    length A (app A l1 l2) = plus (length A l1) (length A l2).
  Proof.
    intros A l1. induction l1; simpl; auto.
    rewrite IHl1. reflexivity.
  Qed.
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\begin{description}
  \item[Extraction] Coq can extract verified programs to OCaml, Haskell, or Scheme. The extraction erases types and proof terms, producing efficient executable code.
  \item[SSReflect] A proof language extension that provides a more structured, concise style of proof scripting. Widely used in the Mathematical Components library.
  \item[Equations] A plugin that provides a high-level interface for defining functions by pattern matching, with automatic generation of equations and termination proofs.
\end{description}

\subsection{Lean Extended: Metaprogramming and Mathlib}

Lean 4 is notable for being \emph{self-hosted}: the Lean compiler is written in Lean itself. This enables powerful metaprogramming:

\begin{description}
  \item[Elab Monad] Lean's elaboration monad allows users to write custom syntax extensions, tactic scripts, and code generators. The entire Lean front end is accessible to user code.
  \item[Macros] Lean 4 macros operate on syntax trees, enabling domain-specific language extensions without leaving the Lean environment.
  \item[Mathlib] The mathematical library of Lean contains over 100,000 lines of formalized mathematics, covering algebra, analysis, topology, and number theory. It is one of the largest formalized mathematics libraries in existence.
\end{description}

